    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>An <strong>under desk elliptical trainer</strong> is a small exercise machine that sits on the floor under your desk. You stay in your chair, put your feet on two pedals and move them through a smooth oval path while you type, read, take calls or watch something. It is the seated, desk-sized cousin of the elliptical you see at the gym.</p>
<p>If you are new to the idea, the category can be confusing. The same kind of machine is sold as an under desk exerciser, a leg exerciser, under desk pedals, desk exercise equipment or office workout equipment, and those labels cover several quite different products.</p>
<p>This beginner's guide explains how the machine works, the four main types of under desk exercise equipment, what each is good for, and how to choose. If you would rather skip to a recommendation, the quiz further down takes about a minute.</p>

<h2>What Is an Under Desk Elliptical Trainer?</h2>
<p>At its simplest, it is a box with two pedals. Inside the box is a flywheel connected to the pedals by a crank. As you push, the pedals rise, move forward, drop and slide back, which is the elliptical motion. The flywheel keeps that motion smooth, and a resistance system controls how hard each stride feels.</p>
<p>What makes it different from other exercise equipment is that it is designed for <em>sitting</em>. It is low enough for your knees to clear the desk, quiet enough to use while you work, and gentle enough that you can keep going for long stretches without getting out of breath. It is not built for a hard 30-minute workout. It is built for adding movement to the hours you already spend sitting.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>In one sentence:</strong> this kind of elliptical machine under desk turns sitting time into light, low-impact leg activity without needing extra time in your day.</p></div>

<h2>How an Under Desk Elliptical Trainer Works</h2>
<h3>The elliptical motion</h3>
<p>Unlike a bike, where your feet travel in a circle, an elliptical keeps your feet on flat pedals that glide through an oval. Your knee bends less sharply at the top, which is why many people with sensitive knees find it more comfortable than a desk bike.</p>
<h3>Resistance</h3>
<p>Resistance sets how hard you work. Most good machines use <strong>magnetic</strong> resistance: magnets sit close to the flywheel without touching it, and a knob moves them closer for more effort. It is quiet and does not wear out. Cheaper machines may use a friction pad or strap, which is louder. Our guide to the <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-with-resistance/">under desk elliptical with resistance</a> explains each type and how many levels you need.</p>
<h3>Manual or motorized drive</h3>
<p>On a manual machine, your legs drive the pedals. On a motorized machine, a plug-in motor moves them for you at a set speed, and you can push along for more effort.</p>
<h3>The display</h3>
<p>Most models have a small screen showing time, strides and estimated calories. Some, such as the [[amazon:cubii-jr2-plus|Cubii JR2+]], sync with a phone app over Bluetooth.</p>

<h2>Types of Under Desk Exercise Equipment</h2>
<p>When people search for an under desk exerciser or desk exercise equipment, they usually mean one of these four types.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>How you use it</th><th>Best for</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Manual seated elliptical</td><td>Seated, you drive the pedals</td><td>Most desk workers, calorie goals, quiet offices</td><td>You need to pedal under your own power</td></tr>
<tr><td>Motorized seated elliptical</td><td>Seated, a motor moves the pedals</td><td>Limited mobility, seniors, circulation goals</td><td>Needs an outlet, low burn in passive mode</td></tr>
<tr><td>Under desk bike (pedals)</td><td>Seated, circular pedaling</td><td>Higher resistance, cyclists</td><td>More knee bend, may need more desk clearance</td></tr>
<tr><td>Walking pad or strider</td><td>Standing</td><td>Real steps at a standing desk</td><td>Needs a standing desk, louder, harder to type</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<h3>Manual seated elliptical</h3>
<p>The most popular type, and the one most people picture when they hear "elliptical for under desk." The [[amazon:cubii-jr2]] and [[amazon:deskcycle-ellipse]] are the best-known examples. Read our <a href="/manual-under-desk-elliptical/">manual under desk elliptical</a> guide for the full rundown.</p>
<h3>Motorized seated elliptical</h3>
<p>Also sold as automatic or electric ellipticals. The motor moves your legs, which makes them popular with older adults and people with limited strength. The [[amazon:ancheer|ANCHEER motorized elliptical]] is a common choice. Our <a href="/automatic-under-desk-elliptical/">automatic under desk elliptical</a> guide explains passive and active modes.</p>
<h3>Under desk bike or pedals</h3>
<p>Mini exercise bikes like the [[amazon:deskcycle-2|DeskCycle 2]] use a circular pedal stroke. They can offer more resistance, but your knees travel higher. See <a href="/under-desk-elliptical-vs-bike/">under desk elliptical vs bike</a>.</p>
<h3>Walking pads and standing striders</h3>
<p>These are for standing desks. A walking pad gives you real steps but needs a desk you can raise, and it is harder to type while walking. A compact strider like the Stamina InMotion E1000 is used standing with something to hold. Neither works for seated use.</p>

<h2>What a Seated Elliptical Can Do for You</h2>
<p>Set expectations right and you will be happy with your purchase.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Add activity without adding time.</strong> This is the main benefit. The CDC and WHO recommend at least 150 minutes of moderate activity a week, and pedaling while you work can cover a good chunk of that.</li>
<li><strong>Burn some extra calories.</strong> Light to moderate seated pedaling burns roughly 100 to 200+ calories an hour for most people, depending on weight and effort.</li>
<li><strong>Ease stiffness.</strong> Owners often report that their legs feel less stiff and restless after a long day.</li>
<li><strong>Stay low impact.</strong> No pounding on knees, hips or ankles.</li>
</ul>
<p>What it will not do: replace strength training, give you an upper-body workout, or cause weight loss on its own.</p>

<h2>Beginner Glossary: The Terms You Will See</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Under desk exerciser / leg exerciser under desk:</strong> a catch-all for any seated pedal machine, elliptical or bike.</li>
<li><strong>Under desk pedals:</strong> usually a mini bike with a circular stroke, sometimes used loosely for ellipticals too.</li>
<li><strong>Stride:</strong> one full loop of the pedal. Displays often count strides rather than steps.</li>
<li><strong>Flywheel:</strong> the spinning weight inside that keeps the motion smooth. Heavier generally feels smoother.</li>
<li><strong>Passive mode:</strong> on motorized machines, the motor moves your legs and you relax.</li>
<li><strong>Clearance:</strong> the space between the top of your thighs at the highest pedal point and the underside of your desk.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Using Desk Exercise Equipment at the Office</h2>
<p>Bringing office workout equipment into a shared workspace raises a few questions a home office never does. A quiet manual elliptical is usually the easiest sell, because it needs no outlet, takes up only the space under your own desk and makes little noise.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ask first.</strong> Some workplaces have rules about personal equipment, so a quick check with facilities avoids surprises.</li>
<li><strong>Think about neighbors.</strong> Magnetic resistance is close to silent; motors and friction pads are not. Try it during a quiet moment before your first meeting.</li>
<li><strong>Keep it tidy.</strong> A mat under the machine protects carpet tiles and stops it from wandering into the walkway.</li>
<li><strong>Consider a lighter model</strong> if you split time between home and the office. Our <a href="/portable-elliptical-under-desk/">portable elliptical under desk</a> guide covers machines built for carrying.</li>
</ul>
<p>Short on space under a small office desk? A <a href="/mini-elliptical-under-desk/">mini elliptical under desk</a> has the smallest footprint, at the cost of a shorter stride.</p>

<h2>Common Beginner Mistakes</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Starting with too much resistance.</strong> Heavy settings make it hard to focus and leave your legs tired by lunchtime. Add time before you add resistance.</li>
<li><strong>Sitting too close.</strong> If your knees brush the desk, slide the machine forward a few inches or lower your chair a notch.</li>
<li><strong>Ignoring the chair.</strong> A rolling chair drifts backward with every push. Lock it in place before you blame the machine.</li>
<li><strong>Trusting the calorie display.</strong> Built-in counters are estimates. Use our <a href="/tools/under-desk-elliptical-calorie-calculator/">calorie calculator</a> for a number based on your weight and effort.</li>
<li><strong>Using it only when you remember.</strong> Tie sessions to something you already do, such as every call or every email batch.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How to Choose an Under Desk Elliptical Trainer</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Measure your knee clearance.</strong> Your knees rise at the top of each stride, and hitting the desk is the most common reason for returns. Try our <a href="/tools/desk-clearance-checker/">desk clearance checker</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Decide manual or motorized.</strong> Can you pedal comfortably? Go manual. Is mobility or strength limited? Consider motorized.</li>
<li><strong>Prioritize quiet if you take calls.</strong> Magnetic resistance is the quietest.</li>
<li><strong>Check the resistance range.</strong> Enough levels to start easy and progress.</li>
<li><strong>Think about weight.</strong> Heavier machines slide less; lighter ones are easier to move between rooms.</li>
<li><strong>Look at brand support.</strong> Replacement parts and a clear warranty matter over years of use.</li>
</ol>
