<h2>Your First Week of Desk Pedaling</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Days 1 to 2:</strong> two or three 10-minute sessions at the lowest resistance. Focus on finding a seat position where your knees clear the desk comfortably.</li>
<li><strong>Days 3 to 5:</strong> stretch to 15 to 20 minutes per session. Try pedaling during calls and while reading.</li>
<li><strong>Days 6 to 7:</strong> add one notch of resistance for part of a session. Try a few minutes pedaling backward.</li>
</ul>
<p>Two setup tips will save you frustration: put a [[amazon:cubii-mat|non-slip mat]] under the machine so it does not creep, and use [[amazon:caster-cups|caster cups]] or a chair without wheels so you do not push yourself away from the desk. Our step-by-step guide on <a href="/how-to-use-under-desk-elliptical/">how to use an under desk elliptical</a> covers posture and chair height in detail.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Health note:</strong> if you have a heart condition, a joint replacement, a circulation problem or balance issues, check with your doctor before starting any new exercise, including seated pedaling.</p></div>

<h2>Is an Under Desk Elliptical Trainer Worth It?</h2>
<p>For anyone who sits for most of the day, a seated elliptical is one of the easiest ways to move more without changing your schedule. It will not replace the gym, but it fills hours that would otherwise be completely still. Start with a quiet manual model like the [[amazon:cubii-jr2]], or the budget [[amazon:sunny-sf-e3872|Sunny Health &amp; Fitness under desk elliptical]] if you want to try the idea first.</p>
